<!DOCTYPE html><html xmlns:o="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:office" xmlns:w="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:word" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/TR/REC-html40"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><title>literature-essays-plays</title><style>table{border-collapse:collapse;width:100%}td,th{border:1px solid #333;padding:6px}th{background:#eee}img{max-width:100%}</style></head><body><h1>literature-essays-plays</h1><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li>Literature — Essays and Plays</li></ul><h2>Literature — Essays and Plays</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Concept: Five essays argue (Jobs on death as life&#x27;s clarifier, Parker on poverty as lived experience, Haldane on what life requires), three one-act plays compress full drama into one room and one crisis (Trifles&#x27; women reading what men miss, A Sunny Morning&#x27;s bench romance, Refund&#x27;s satire of schooling). Every answer runs theme → evidence (quoted moment) → technique → writer&#x27;s purpose.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Formula: answer spine: claim (theme in one line) → moment (quoted scene/line) → technique (device, irony, dialogue) → purpose (what the writer makes you feel/decide) · essay tools: thesis, anecdote, rhetoric · play tools: dialogue, stage business, dramatic irony</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Conditions: Claims need textual moments — unquoted assertions score thin. Separate the writer from the speaker (Jobs the speaker ≠ Jobs the man; narrators can mislead). One-act analysis stays inside the single setting and timespan; importing other works dilutes.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Special cases: Daudet&#x27;s &#x27;Last Lesson&#x27; classroom-exit moment (mother&#x27;s eyes leaving her son — NEB 2024, 5 marks): read gesture as theme (language = identity under occupation). Trifles turns &#x27;trifles&#x27; (preserves, stitching) into the murder&#x27;s evidence — the title is the thesis. Satire (Refund, Parker) argues through exaggeration; answer the target, not just the joke.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Solved: Last-Lesson moment answer: claim — M. Hamel&#x27;s final lesson makes language the homeland; moment — the mother&#x27;s gaze torn from her son by the order; technique — Daudet freezes a gesture instead of explaining it; purpose — occupation steals even farewells, so the reader feels linguistic loss bodily (NEB 2024, 5-mark pattern).</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Limitation: Theme-hunting flattens texts into morals — examiners reward observed detail over slogans. Author intention is partly unknowable; argue from the text, not biography. One framework cannot fit satire and elegy identically — adjust the lens per genre.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Derivation: Build any answer from the text outward: list striking moments → cluster them into a theme → name the technique carrying each (irony in Refund&#x27;s exam-farce, symbolism in Trifles&#x27; birdcage, rhetoric in Jobs&#x27;s three stories) → state the purpose as effect-on-reader. Four moves, any essay or play.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Shortcut: Titles are theses (Trifles, Refund, What is Poverty?). Endings reveal purpose — answer from the last scene backward. Always quote: one short exact line beats a paragraph of paraphrase. Device + effect, never device alone.</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Board question: NEB 2024 (5): Last Lesson exit moment — gesture as meaning (literature bank). NEB 2023 (2): device ID — &#x27;wind whispered&#x27; = personification. Essay-theme and play-character questions follow the claim→moment→technique→purpose spine.</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Syllabus topics: Essay 1 — Sharing Tradition (Frank LaPena); Essay 2 — How to Live Before You Die (Steve Jobs); Essay 3 — What I Require From Life (J.B.S. Haldane); Essay 4 — What is Poverty? (Jo Goodwin Parker); Essay 5 — Scientific Research is a Token of Humankind&#x27;s Survival (Vladimir Keilis-Borok); One-Act Play 1 — Trifles (Susan Glaspell); One-Act Play 2 — A Sunny Morning (Serafín and Joaquín Álvarez Quintero); One-Act Play 3 — Refund (Fritz Karinthy)</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Syllabus: Essay 1 — Sharing Tradition (Frank LaPena)</td></tr><tr><td>Syllabus: Essay 2 — How to Live Before You Die (Steve Jobs)</td></tr><tr><td>Syllabus: Essay 3 — What I Require From Life (J.B.S. Haldane)</td></tr><tr><td>Syllabus: Essay 4 — What is Poverty? (Jo Goodwin Parker)</td></tr><tr><td>Syllabus: Essay 5 — Scientific Research is a Token of Humankind&#x27;s Survival (Vladimir Keilis-Borok)</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Essays and one-act plays — theme, character, moment</p><hr><h2>Comparison Tables</h2><h3>Concept Comparison</h3><table><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Description</th></tr><tr><td>1: Literature — Essays and Plays</td><td>Essays and one-act plays — theme, character, moment</td></tr></table></body></html>